Guided Exercise: Provision Persistent Data Volumes
Goal of the Lab
Deploy a MySQL database with persistent storage so that:
•	Data survives pod deletion
•	Storage is provided by Persistent Volume (PV)
•	Requested using Persistent Volume Claim (PVC)
You will also identify:
•	The PV backing the database
•	The storage provisioner
________________________________________
Step 1: Prepare Lab Environment
Run:
lab start storage-volumes
What this command does
It prepares the OpenShift lab environment:
•	Creates project storage-volumes
•	Ensures cluster access
•	Prepares required files
Example file used later:
init-db.sql
________________________________________
Step 2: Login to OpenShift Web Console
Open browser:
https://console-openshift-console.apps.ocp4.example.com
Login:
Username: developer
Password: developer
Switch perspective:
Administrator
Why?
Because administrator view allows access to:
•	Storage
•	Deployments
•	PVC
•	PV
•	Services
________________________________________
Step 3: Identify Default Storage Class
Go to:
Storage → StorageClasses
You will see something like:
Name	Provisioner
nfs-storage	k8s-sigs.io/nfs-subdir-external-provisioner
Important detail:
Default StorageClass = nfs-storage
What this means
If a PVC does not specify a storage class, Kubernetes automatically uses:
nfs-storage
Provisioner used:
k8s-sigs.io/nfs-subdir-external-provisioner
This means:
Storage comes from an NFS server
________________________________________
Step 4: Create MySQL Deployment
Go to:
Workloads → Deployments → Create Deployment
Fill values:
Field	Value
Deployment Name	db-pod
Image	registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/rhel8/mysql-80
Project	storage-volumes
Image used:
rhel8/mysql-80
This is MySQL 8 container.
________________________________________
Step 5: Add Environment Variables
MySQL requires configuration variables.
Variable	Value
MYSQL_USER	user1
MYSQL_PASSWORD	redhat123
MYSQL_DATABASE	items
These variables:
•	create database
•	create user
•	configure authentication
________________________________________
Step 6: Set Replicas
Set:
Replicas = 1
Reason:
Database should not run multiple replicas without clustering.
________________________________________
Step 7: Create Service for MySQL
Go to:
Networking → Services → Create Service
Fill values:
Field	Value
Name	db-pod
Selector	app=db-pod
Port	3306
Target Port	3306
What this does
Creates internal access for MySQL.
Flow:
Application
     ↓
Service (db-pod)
     ↓
MySQL Pod
Port:
3306
Default MySQL port.
________________________________________
Step 8: Add Persistent Storage (PVC)
Now we add storage to database.
Go to:
Workloads → Deployments → db-pod
Click:
Add Storage
Then:
Create new claim
Fill values:
Field	Value
PVC Name	db-pod-pvc
Access Mode	RWO
Size	1 GiB
Volume Mode	Filesystem
Mount Path	/var/lib/mysql
________________________________________
Why /var/lib/mysql?
MySQL stores database files in:
/var/lib/mysql
So mounting storage here ensures:
Database files persist
________________________________________
Result
OpenShift creates:
PVC → db-pod-pvc
PV → pvc-xxxxx
________________________________________
Step 9: Identify the PV
Click:
db-pod-pvc
Open:
YAML tab
Look for:
.spec.volumeName
Example:
volumeName: pvc-8b08a...
Even though name starts with pvc, it is actually a PersistentVolume.
________________________________________
Step 10: Verify Deployment Updated
Go to:
Workloads → Deployments → db-pod → YAML
You will see new configuration.
Volumes section
volumes:
- name: db-pod-pvc
  persistentVolumeClaim:
    claimName: db-pod-pvc
________________________________________
Volume Mount
volumeMounts:
- name: db-pod-pvc
  mountPath: /var/lib/mysql
Meaning:
PVC storage → mounted into MySQL container
________________________________________
Step 11: Initialize Database Using ConfigMap
Database needs initial data.
File:
init-db.sql
Contents:
CREATE TABLE Item
INSERT rows
________________________________________
Step 12: Create ConfigMap
Run:
oc create configmap init-db-cm \
--from-file ~/DO180/labs/storage-volumes/configmap/init-db.sql
Command Explanation
Part	Meaning
create configmap	create configuration object
init-db-cm	name
--from-file	use file content
This stores SQL script inside ConfigMap.
________________________________________
Step 13: Mount ConfigMap in Deployment
Run:
oc set volumes deployment/db-pod \
--add \
--name init-db-volume \
--type configmap \
--configmap-name init-db-cm \
--mount-path /var/db/config
Meaning:
SQL script → mounted inside container
Location:
/var/db/config/init-db.sql
________________________________________
Step 14: Execute SQL Script
Enter container shell:
oc rsh deployment/db-pod
Run SQL:
mysql -uuser1 -predhat123 items < /var/db/config/init-db.sql
This:
•	creates table
•	inserts data
________________________________________
Step 15: Verify Database Data
Run:
mysql -uuser1 -predhat123 items \
-e 'select * from Item;'
Output:
+----+-------------------+------+
| id | description       | done |
+----+-------------------+------+
| 1  | Pick up newspaper | 0x00 |
| 2  | Buy groceries     | 0x01 |
+----+-------------------+------+
Database initialized successfully.
________________________________________
Step 16: Test Persistence
Delete deployment:
oc delete deployment/db-pod
Check PVC:
oc get pvc
Output:
db-pod-pvc   Bound
PVC still exists.
Meaning:
Storage still exists
Data not deleted
________________________________________
Step 17: Recreate Deployment
Create deployment again:
oc create deployment db-pod \
--port 3306 \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/rhel8/mysql-80
Add environment variables:
oc set env deployment/db-pod \
MYSQL_USER=user1 \
MYSQL_PASSWORD=redhat123 \
MYSQL_DATABASE=items
________________________________________
Step 18: Reattach PVC
oc set volumes deployment/db-pod \
--add \
--type pvc \
--mount-path /var/lib/mysql \
--name db-pod-vol \
--claim-name db-pod-pvc
Now MySQL uses same storage.
________________________________________
Step 19: Verify Data Still Exists
Run test pod:
oc run query-db -it --rm \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/do180-dbinit \
--restart Never \
-- /bin/bash -c "mysql -uuser1 -predhat123 \
--protocol tcp -h db-pod -P3306 items \
-e 'select * from Item;'"
Result:
Pick up newspaper
Buy groceries
This proves:
Data persisted
Even after deleting pod.
________________________________________
Step 20: Cleanup Resources
Delete deployment:
oc delete deployment/db-pod
Delete PVC:
oc delete pvc/db-pod-pvc
________________________________________
Step 21: Finish Lab
Run:
lab finish storage-volumes
This cleans up environment.
________________________________________
Architecture of This Lab
MySQL Pod
     │
     ▼
PVC (db-pod-pvc)
     │
     ▼
PV (pvc-xxxx)
     │
     ▼
StorageClass (nfs-storage)
     │
     ▼
NFS Storage Server
________________________________________
Key Concepts Learned
Concept	Explanation
PVC	storage request
PV	actual storage
StorageClass	storage type
Mount path	location in container
Persistent storage	survives pod deletion
